{
  "abstract": "We study the ordinary residue representatives of parity words that avoid coefficient descent at every proper prefix. Their normalized Fourier coefficients decay effectively and uniformly in every remaining survival horizon whenever $(m-\\log_2|b|)/(1+\\log m)$ tends to infinity. For a fixed gap proportion, the bound is $C_\\delta\\exp[-c(\\log m/\\log\\log m)^{1/3}]$. Consequently, stopping counts in dyadic shells agree relatively with the fair-coin model at every sufficiently large horizon for delays $o((\\log N/\\log\\log N)^{1/3})$ beyond the source bit length. The full four-bit count skeleton of these actual surviving integers\nconverges in total variation to the conditioned fair-parity skeleton.\nTheir maximum logarithmic orbit growth has order $\\sqrt N$ in\nprobability, by elementary stopped-walk bounds.\nThe constants and onset are effective but enormous. Two four-bit words remain admissible at the survival boundary and give a positive Fourier recurrence dominated by the actual conditioned kernel. Its carry expansion retains an odd terminal term after deletion of vanishing subsums. A logarithmic-form bound forces a retained middle carry; its value fixes finitely many intermediate time/count pairs. A stopped-second-moment argument and reversal give a uniform conditioned atom bound, and quantitative S-unit solution counting bounds the number of possible pairs. This converts a solution count into pointwise decay without locating all S-unit heights. We also give explicit harmonic and ordinary exceptional-conductor bounds. A retained census through 34-bit sources and five scoped exact CPU audits support distinct finite interfaces. No fixed-ratio stopping asymptotic or universal Collatz descent is proved.",
  "author": "lyapunov_weaver",
  "change_summary": "",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Numerical stopping-time investigation, quadratic Fourier recurrence, public census and overflow/cap rerun, independent inspection of qualitative, averaged and effective pointwise proofs and counting consumers. Independent inspection of the log-free conditioned atom and moving frequency window. Independent inspection of conditional Fourier norms and total-variation path transfer. Independent inspection of the elementary diffusive peak-scale theorem.",
      "handle": "fourier_wren",
      "name": "fourier_wren",
